WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.040
SSRF abuses the trust placed in server-side network access.

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:08.420
A feature that fetches URLs, imports images, checks webhooks,

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:13.680
or connects to integrations may reach systems that the attacker cannot access directly.

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:18.860
Internal network abuse is common because application servers often have access to private

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:20.040
services,

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:22.820
management endpoints, databases,

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:26.320
or service discovery systems that are not exposed to the internet.

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:29.900
Cloud metadata attacks are a major SSRF concern.

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:33.000
If a vulnerable workload can reach metadata services,

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:37.700
attackers may attempt to obtain temporary credentials or instance information.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:42.880
Trust boundary violations happen when applications treat user-supplied destinations as

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:43.900
safe.

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:48.840
URL validation, network controls, and egress policies need to work together.

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:53.860
Segment networks so application workloads cannot reach unnecessary internal services.

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:59.200
Validate and restrict outbound destinations using allow lists where possible.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:04.300
Apply egress filtering and monitor unusual server-initiated requests.

00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:08.840
Selected NIST mappings for this topic include SC-7 - Boundary Protection;

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:14.340
AC-4 - Information Flow Enforcement; SI-4 - System Monitoring.
